# NPIN architecture

NPIN is a local Windows desktop shared brain. It scans repositories the user chooses and shows them as one graph of repositories, services, APIs, databases, packages, skills, agents, rules, and concepts. A canon memory sits beside that graph so a new project can inherit skills, rules, and architecture patterns.

Stack, fixed until the user changes it:

- App shell: Tauri 2, React, TypeScript, Vite. Files stay on this machine.
- Code graph: local Graphify CLI (`graphifyy`). It writes `graphify-out/graph.json` per repo. NPIN reads that file. Do not reimplement a parser. Do not upload private repos to hosted Graphify. The hosted Graphify plugin was declined; the local CLI is the scanner.
- Canon store: one SQLite file in app data. No Neo4j and no Docker.
- Graph view: sigma.js and graphology. A heavier renderer waits until a real graph is too big.
- Graphiti is not the v1 memory. Revisit it only in Phase 6 if a Windows-local backend is verified.

Edges are extracted (code, manifests, Cursor files) or canon (decisions kept on purpose). Writing skills or rules into another repository happens only after the user confirms that repository.

`graphify-out/graph.json` exists for the model files. Query it first, then open the specific lines you need to change. The SQL files are not in that graph; `model/schema.sql` is the source of truth for kinds and links.
